# direnv entry point (https://direnv.net) — loaded on `cd` into this repo, so the dev shell AND
# every `nix run .#<app>` see the same project-scoped environment without touching global secrets.
#
# Run `direnv allow` once after cloning (and again whenever this file changes — that prompt is
# direnv refusing to execute a file you have not read, which is the point of it).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: switching between projects used to mean re-pointing global credentials —
# `gcloud config set project`, a different VERCEL_TOKEN in the shell profile, and so on. With this,
# each repo carries its own values and they vanish again when you `cd` out.

use flake

# Project env files, LOWEST precedence first — direnv's `dotenv` lets a later file override an
# earlier one, so this ordering deliberately mirrors Next.js's own precedence
# (.env.development.local > .env.local > .env). All three are gitignored; none belongs in git.
dotenv_if_exists .env
dotenv_if_exists .env.local
dotenv_if_exists .env.development.local

# Per-operator overrides, highest precedence, never committed (.envrc.local is gitignored). This is
# a SHELL file, not a dotenv one, so it can compute values and call out to a password manager
# rather than storing a secret in plaintext. For example:
#
#   export CLOUDSDK_CORE_ACCOUNT=someone-else@example.com
#   export CLOUDSDK_CORE_PROJECT=my-sandbox
#   export VERCEL_TOKEN="$(security find-generic-password -s vercel-token -w)"
#
source_env_if_exists .envrc.local
